## Statement

A relay channel $(\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}_r,\; p(y, y_r \mid x, x_r),\;
\mathcal{Y} \times \mathcal{Y}_r)$ has a sender $X$, a receiver observing $Y$, and a
relay that observes $Y_r$ and transmits $X_r$ (causally, with $X_{r,i}$ a function of
$Y_r^{i-1}$) to help.

**Determine the capacity $C$ — a single-letter characterization — of the general
discrete memoryless relay channel.** The Gaussian case with fixed channel gains is
already open.

**Formal version.** The Lean statement asks whether the best known single-letter lower bound
is the capacity. For every finite discrete memoryless relay channel
$p(y, y_r \mid x, x_r)$,
$$
C = R_{\rm CM},
$$
where:
- $C$ = `relayCapacity` is the operational capacity. Codes use a deterministic encoder,
  strictly causal deterministic relay maps $x_{r,i} = f_i(y_r^{i-1})$, a deterministic decoder,
  $\max(1, \lfloor 2^{nR}\rfloor)$ messages and average error.
- $R_{\rm CM}$ = `chongMotaniRate` is the Chong–Motani (2011, Theorem 2) partial
  decode-forward + compress-forward rate, in the equivalent form (7) of
  El Gamal–Gohari–Nair:
$$
R_{\rm CM} = \sup\ \min\{c_1, c_2, c_3\},
$$
$$
\begin{aligned}
c_1 &= I(X, X_r; Y) - I(Y_r; V \mid S, U, X, X_r, Y),\\
c_2 &= I(X; V, Y \mid S, U, X_r) + I(U; Y_r \mid S, X_r),\\
c_3 &= I(X; Y \mid S, U, X_r) + I(U; Y_r \mid S, X_r) + I(X_r; Y \mid S, U)
       - I(Y_r; V \mid S, U, X, X_r, Y).
\end{aligned}
$$
The supremum is over finite alphabets for $S, U, V$ and all joint laws of the form
$$
p(s, u)\, p(x \mid s, u)\, p(x_r \mid s)\, p(y, y_r \mid x, x_r)\, p(v \mid s, u, x_r, y_r).
$$
Here $S$ is the cooperative information known to both source and relay (El Gamal–Gohari–Nair
call it $W$), $U$ is the part of the message the relay decodes, and $V$ is the relay's
compression of $Y_r$. Given $(S, U)$, the inputs $X$ and $X_r$ are conditionally independent,
because the source does not know the relay's compression index. $R_{\rm CM}$ contains
Cover–El Gamal Theorem 7 (replace $I(X_r; Y \mid S, U)$ by $I(X_r; Y \mid S)$ in $c_3$), and
hence direct transmission, decode-forward, partial decode-forward, compress-forward, and all
of these with coded time-sharing.

The inequality $R_{\rm CM} \le C$ is the published achievability theorem; it is not
formalized. The open content is the converse $C \le R_{\rm CM}$. A proof would settle the
problem. A disproof needs a coding scheme that provably beats every known single-letter
scheme on some finite channel. Either would be a major result. The statement is believed open
(see the notes on the formalization). It may well be false: there is no structural reason
for the best known scheme to be optimal, but no counterexample is known.

## Background

The three-node relay channel is the smallest network in which *cooperation* appears,
and it is the building block of every multi-hop and cooperative scheme in modern
wireless systems. Cover and El Gamal's 1979 paper introduced decode-and-forward and
compress-and-forward, the two strategies that (with their descendants, including noisy
network coding) still bracket what is achievable. That a three-node memoryless network
has unknown capacity after fifty years is the cleanest illustration of how hard network
information theory is.

## What is known

- Capacity is known for degraded and reversely degraded relay channels, and with
  feedback (Cover–El Gamal 1979); for semideterministic relay channels
  (El Gamal–Aref 1982); and for some classes with orthogonal components
  (El Gamal–Zahedi).
- The cutset bound (Cover–El Gamal) is the standard upper bound; it is **not tight in
  general** — Zhang (1988) showed a gap for a class of channels. Wu–Barnes–Özgür
  (2019) resolved Cover's 1987 question for the Gaussian primitive relay channel via
  high-dimensional isoperimetry: $C(C_0) < C(\infty)$ for **every finite** relay-link
  rate $C_0$, at every SNR — the critical rate is infinite. Reverse-hypercontractivity
  proofs followed (Liu–Özgür 2018), and El Gamal–Gohari–Nair (2022) gave a
  strengthened cutset bound by classical converse techniques, strictly tighter for
  Gaussian relay channels with nonzero gains and for binary symmetric relay channels,
  resolving Kim's conjecture for orthogonal-receiver relay channels.
- For the primitive relay channel with BSC components, capacity is known exactly for
  $R_0 \leq I(X;Z) - I(X;Y)$ (decode-forward meets the cutset bound) and
  $R_0 \geq H(Z\mid Y)$ (compress-forward meets it); in the interior interval the
  evaluated achievable rates and the cutset bound separate by up to ~0.1 bit in the
  repository's examples. An attempt to numerically maximize the
  El Gamal–Gohari–Nair converse objective produced candidate objective values, but
  these are lower estimates of a maximization-defined upper bound and therefore do
  not certify a tighter capacity upper bound. For the symmetric benchmark
  $\delta_1=\delta_2=0.1$, $R_0=0.2$, a later branch-and-bound evaluation of the
  specialized EGN theorem does certify
  $0.606432<C<0.649557$, improving the cutset upper bound $0.731004$. A
  fixed-input-bias concavity lemma reduces this certificate to a one-dimensional
  interval calculation (see attempt log).
- Best general achievability: combinations of partial decode-forward and
  compress-forward; noisy network coding (Lim–Kim–El Gamal–Chung 2011) recovers and
  extends compress-forward to networks. None matches the best known converses in
  general.
  - Cover–El Gamal Theorem 7 is stated in its three-constraint form by Chong–Motani
    (IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory 57(3), 2011, Lemma 2). Their Theorem 2 is at least as large,
    and they conjectured it can be strictly larger. El Gamal–Gohari–Nair ("Achievable rates
    for the relay channel with orthogonal receiver components") showed the two coincide
    for orthogonal receiver components, simplified the expression, and showed the
    Mondelli–Hassani–Urbanke (2019) scheme is subsumed by it. They also showed that
    time-sharing strictly improves pure compress-forward, which disproves an Ahlswede–Han
    conjecture.
  - We know of no standard DM relay channel on which a scheme provably beats Theorem 7, let
    alone the Chong–Motani bound. Wu (arXiv:1504.04515) beats Theorem 7 only with
    relay-to-source feedback, or when the source observes the relay's signal.
  - Ponniah (arXiv:2609.15709, 2026, unrefereed) claims $C=\max\{R_{\rm DF},R_{\rm U\text{-}CF},R_{\rm C\text{-}CF}\}$.
    This is refuted by the primitive-BSC time-sharing example, viewed as a DM relay channel
    with an orthogonal BSC link of capacity $0.3$: capacity is at least $0.5396$, while all
    three of Ponniah's rates are at most $0.5310$. The paper's definitions (82)–(97) are
    partly inconsistent. This bound reads each rate under the same joint law as its own
    feasibility constraint (see attempt log). Shiu (arXiv:2609.18727, 2026, unrefereed)
    gives an independent counterexample, in which partial decode-forward (a special case of
    Theorem 7) beats Ponniah's formula.
- Approximate capacity: for Gaussian relay networks, capacity is known within a
  constant gap (Avestimehr–Diggavi–Tse deterministic-model program).

## Notes on the formalization (for expert review)

- **Statement fix, 2026-11-27.**
  - *What was wrong.* The previous right-hand side, `coverElGamalTheorem7Rate`, was a
    mis-transcription of Cover–El Gamal Theorem 7 (CEG79, (83a–c)). It dropped the auxiliary
    $V$ of (83b), allowed an arbitrary joint law $p(u, x, x_r)$ instead of the factorisation
    (83b), and dropped the constraint (83c). This credited compress-forward with coherent
    source and relay inputs. On a 4×2→6×2 channel, the relay observes the destination's noise
    and the destination sees a binary adder $X_2+X_r$. There the capacity is exactly $3/2$ (by
    Fano), but the old functional equals $\log_2 3$. So the old statement was false
    (`attempts/relay-channel/2026-11-26-claude-opus-5-5-thm7-transcription-catalog-error.md`,
    verified by an independent reviewer).
  - *What changed.* The right-hand side is now `chongMotaniRate`, given above. It is built on
    the joint law `chongMotaniJoint`, which enforces $X \perp X_r \mid (S, U)$. All three rate
    constraints are present. Form (7) is equal to Chong–Motani Theorem 2 as restated by
    El Gamal–Gohari–Nair (Theorem 2, Remark 1). We checked both inclusions:
    - from Theorem 2 to (7), put $S=(W,Q)$ and $U'=(U,W,Q)$;
    - from (7) to Theorem 2, put $Q=\emptyset$, $W=S$ and $U'=(U,S)$;
    - each conditional mutual information is unchanged, because $(X, Y, Y_r, V)$ is
      independent of $W$ given $(U, Q, X_r)$. The one exception is $I(X, X_r; Y \mid Q)$,
      which becomes $I(X, X_r; Y)$ and can only increase;
    - the third constraint is rewritten with $V - (S, U, X_r, Y_r) - (X, Y)$.

    The operational side `relayCapacity`, the alphabets and the quantifiers are unchanged.
  - *Why Chong–Motani Theorem 2 rather than Theorem 7.* Theorem 2 is never smaller than
    Theorem 7 (CM11, Remark 6). The two are equal on channels with orthogonal receiver
    components (EGN, Theorem 3). Chong and Motani conjectured, from Gaussian numerics, that
    Theorem 2 can be strictly larger. If Theorem 7 were frozen, a proof of that conjecture
    would refute the statement by comparing two achievable rates. Freezing the larger bound
    means a refutation needs a scheme that beats the best known single-letter bound.
  - *Sanity checks.* Let $W_\star$ be the catalog-error channel. Then
    $3/2 = R_{\rm Thm7}(W_\star) \le R_{\rm CM}(W_\star) \le C(W_\star) = 3/2$, where the
    last inequality is the Chong–Motani achievability theorem. The explicit
    point has $S, U$ constant, $X_1, X_2, X_r$ i.i.d. uniform and $V = Y_r$, and gives
    $(c_1, c_2, c_3) = (1.5, 2, 1.5)$. The former coherent point, now with $X_r = S$, gives
    $\min = h(1/3) \approx 0.918$, because $c_3$ binds. An SLSQP search over
    $|S|, |U|, |V| \le 3$ peaks at exactly $1.5$. For each fixed joint law,
    $R_{\rm CM}$ is at most the cutset value. On paper this follows from chain rules and the
    Markov structure. Numerically, over 3000 random channel/law pairs, the largest excess is
    $2\cdot10^{-15}$. These scripts are not in the repository; they evaluate exactly the
    tuple maps of the Lean definition.
  - *Why the new statement is believed open.* This is the literature checked on 2026-11-27.
    - No known capacity result contradicts it. Every channel class whose capacity is known
      reaches capacity inside Theorem 7. This covers degraded channels (including Sato's
      channel, where $Y_r = X$), reversely degraded, semideterministic, orthogonal sender
      components, the Cover–Kim deterministic class, and the relay channel with feedback (a
      degraded channel). It also covers the Aleksic–Razaghi–Yu modulo-sum class, whose
      capacity is reached by quantize-forward, an instance of Theorem 6.
    - No scheme is known to beat it on a standard finite DM relay channel. Each scheme
      claimed to improve on Theorem 7 is either contained in it, untested, or works in a
      different model:
      - Chong–Motani–Garg 2007 and Chong–Motani 2011: this is the frozen bound itself.
      - Mondelli–Hassani–Urbanke 2019: subsumed (EGN, Lemma 1).
      - El Gamal–Gohari–Nair's time-sharing gain: inside Theorem 7. It refutes only the
        Ahlswede–Han conjecture on pure compress-forward.
      - Lee–Chung NNC-PDF (arXiv:1505.05435): falls back to Theorem 7 for three nodes
        (their Remark 1).
      - Noisy network coding: equals compress-forward for one relay.
      - Kang–Ulukus (arXiv:0710.0900): "may potentially" beat compress-forward; no strict
        example is given.
      - Dabora–Servetto (cs/0605135): their example has multiple relays.
      - Lattice and structured codes for the relay channel (Song–Devroye): they match DF and
        CF, and give gains only in multi-source networks.
      - Wu (arXiv:1504.04515) and Gabbai–Bross: they beat Theorem 7 only with
        relay-to-source feedback, or on an "enhanced" Gaussian channel in which the source
        observes the relay's transmission.
      - Relay-without-delay (instantaneous relaying, El Gamal–Hassanpour–Mammen): a
        different channel model.
      - Ponniah 2026: refuted (attempt log; Shiu 2026).

      We found no multi-letter or structured-code result that beats a single-letter scheme
      on a standard single-relay DM channel.
    - No converse matches it in general. Upper bounds beyond the cutset bound exist: Zhang
      1988; Aleksic–Razaghi–Yu 2009; Tandon–Ulukus 2008; Wu–Özgür–Xie 2017;
      Wu–Barnes–Özgür 2019; Liu–Özgür 2020; Liu 2022; El Gamal–Gohari–Nair 2022. None
      meets $R_{\rm CM}$ outside the classical classes. For example, for the symmetric
      primitive BSC relay channel the certified interval $0.606432 < C < 0.649557$ above
      leaves the question open.

    The statement is therefore open. We do not claim it is true. A negative answer is
    plausible, but it would need a new coding scheme together with a certified upper bound
    on $R_{\rm CM}$ for the channel in question.
  - *Not covered.* Gaussian channels; see `gaussian-relay-channel-capacity`. Degenerate
    empty alphabets make both sides $0$.
- **Certificate** (`Known/Certificates/RelayChannel.lean`):
  - Both sides lie in $[0, \log_2|\mathcal Y|]$, and each `sSup` is over a nonempty set that
    is bounded above (`relayCapacity_nonneg`, `relayCapacity_le_log2_card`,
    `chongMotaniRate_wellPosed`, `chongMotaniRate_nonneg`, `chongMotaniRate_le_log2_card`).
  - $C \ge$ the capacity of every fixed-relay direct link.
  - The cutset converse $C \le$ cutset is in `Bridge` (`relayCapacity_le_cutsetBound`).
  - Achievability $R_{\rm CM} \le C$ is not formalized.

